# Original study notes for each card, written for namastetarotcenter.com.
# (slug, number, name, three words, upright study note, reversed study note, a question to journal)
MAJORS = [
 ('the-fool', 0, 'The Fool', 'leap, beginning, trust', 'A figure walks toward a cliff edge with a small bundle and a dog at its heels. Students often read it as the start of something before the plan is finished: a move, a new subject, a first date. It asks how much you need to know before you begin.', 'Reversed, the step can turn careless or stall completely. Look at whether fear is holding the foot in the air, or whether excitement is skipping over a real risk.', 'Where in my life am I waiting for a guarantee that will never come?'),
 ('the-magician', 1, 'The Magician', 'skill, focus, tools at hand', 'One hand points up and one points down, and all four suit symbols sit on the table. The card is about using what you already have. In a reading it often marks the moment when talent turns into action.', 'Upside down it can point to scattered effort, or to someone using charm to cover thin substance. Ask which tool is lying unused.', 'Which of my skills am I underusing right now?'),
 ('the-high-priestess', 2, 'The High Priestess', 'inner knowing, patience, the unsaid', 'She sits between a dark pillar and a light one with a scroll half hidden in her robe. This is the card of quiet knowledge and information that has not surfaced yet. It suggests listening longer before speaking.', 'Reversed, a hunch gets ignored or a secret starts to cost something. It can also mean you are relying on other people\'s opinions instead of your own read.', 'What do I already sense that I have not said out loud?'),
 ('the-empress', 3, 'The Empress', 'growth, comfort, care', 'A crowned woman rests in a field of ripening grain. The card speaks of nurture: creative projects, the body, the home, relationships that are being fed. Many readers see it as abundance that comes from tending, not luck.', 'Reversed, care can tip into smothering, or the person giving care forgets themselves. It may also show a project that needs feeding again.', 'What in my life grows best when I give it steady attention?'),
 ('the-emperor', 4, 'The Emperor', 'structure, authority, order', 'A stern ruler on a stone throne, with mountains behind him. He stands for rules, plans and the people who enforce them. In a spread he can be a boss, a parent, or your own discipline.', 'Reversed, structure becomes rigidity or control, or the structure is missing altogether. Ask whether the rules serve the goal.', 'Where would a clearer boundary make my life easier?'),
 ('the-hierophant', 5, 'The Hierophant', 'tradition, teaching, belonging', 'A religious figure blesses two followers. The card is about institutions, schools, mentors and shared beliefs. For students it is the card of learning from a lineage before breaking from it.', 'Reversed, it can signal questioning a tradition, leaving a group, or following a rule that no longer fits.', 'Which teacher or tradition shaped how I make decisions?'),
 ('the-lovers', 6, 'The Lovers', 'choice, union, values', 'Two figures stand beneath an angel. Beyond romance, the card is about a decision that reflects what you value most. Many readers ask "what is being chosen here?" before "who is being loved?"', 'Reversed, values clash, a relationship is out of balance, or a choice is being avoided.', 'What would I choose if I acted fully on my values?'),
 ('the-chariot', 7, 'The Chariot', 'drive, direction, willpower', 'A driver holds two sphinxes pulling in different directions with no reins. Progress here comes from will and focus. It is often read as a win that takes effort to steer.', 'Reversed, the direction is lost or force replaces focus. Two goals may be pulling against each other.', 'What two forces in me want different things, and which one is driving?'),
 ('strength', 8, 'Strength', 'gentle courage, patience, self-control', 'A woman calmly closes a lion\'s mouth with her hands. The strength here is soft: patience, compassion and steady nerves. Students sometimes confuse it with The Chariot; this card wins by calm, not force.', 'Reversed, self-doubt or a short temper takes over. It may ask for more kindness toward yourself.', 'Where would patience serve me better than pressure?'),
 ('the-hermit', 9, 'The Hermit', 'solitude, reflection, guidance', 'An old figure stands on a peak holding a lantern. It is the card of stepping back to think, study or recover. It can also show a mentor who lights only the next few steps.', 'Reversed, solitude becomes isolation, or someone avoids the reflection they need.', 'What question would I answer if I gave it a quiet afternoon?'),
 ('wheel-of-fortune', 10, 'Wheel of Fortune', 'cycles, turning points, timing', 'A great wheel turns in the sky, surrounded by four winged figures. It shows change you do not fully control: luck, timing, the season turning. Readers often look at the cards beside it to see which way it is spinning.', 'Reversed, it may point to resisting a change or repeating a cycle.', 'Which pattern in my life keeps coming back around?'),
 ('justice', 11, 'Justice', 'fairness, cause and effect, truth', 'A figure holds scales and an upright sword. The card is about accountability and clear decisions. In practical readings it can relate to contracts, agreements or legal matters, though a reading is never legal advice.', 'Reversed, something feels unfair, or responsibility is being dodged.', 'Where am I being asked to tell myself the plain truth?'),
 ('the-hanged-man', 12, 'The Hanged Man', 'pause, surrender, new view', 'A man hangs calmly by one foot, a halo around his head. Things are on hold, and the pause brings a different point of view. It is one of the first cards students learn to read as useful rather than bad.', 'Reversed, the waiting turns into stalling, or a sacrifice is made for nothing.', 'What would I see if I looked at this problem upside down?'),
 ('death', 13, 'Death', 'ending, clearing, transition', 'A skeleton rides a white horse while a sun rises behind it. The card almost never means a literal death in a reading; it marks the end of a chapter so another can begin.', 'Reversed, an ending drags on or change is being held back.', 'What is finished that I have not yet let go of?'),
 ('temperance', 14, 'Temperance', 'balance, blending, moderation', 'An angel pours water between two cups, one foot on land and one in a pool. The card is about mixing opposites into something that works and taking the middle path.', 'Reversed, things are out of proportion: too much work, too little rest, or the wrong mix.', 'What in my routine needs a better balance?'),
 ('the-devil', 15, 'The Devil', 'attachment, habit, temptation', 'Two figures stand loosely chained before a horned figure. The chains are loose enough to lift off, which is the lesson students remember. It points at habits, pulls and patterns that feel stronger than they are.', 'Reversed, a hold is loosening, or someone is starting to see the pattern clearly.', 'Which habit would I drop if I noticed how loose the chain is?'),
 ('the-tower', 16, 'The Tower', 'sudden change, revelation, collapse', 'Lightning strikes a tower and people fall from it. It shows a shake-up that clears away something unstable. Readers often follow it with "what was built on a weak base?"', 'Reversed, the shake-up is avoided or delayed, or it happens inwardly.', 'What belief of mine would not survive one honest question?'),
 ('the-star', 17, 'The Star', 'hope, renewal, calm', 'A woman pours water under a sky of stars after the storm of The Tower. The card brings healing, quiet optimism and a sense of direction returning.', 'Reversed, hope runs low or faith in yourself wavers.', 'What gives me hope when nothing is certain yet?'),
 ('the-moon', 18, 'The Moon', 'uncertainty, dreams, illusion', 'A path runs between two towers under a moon, with a dog and a wolf howling. Not everything is clear. The card speaks of intuition, anxiety, dreams and things that look different in daylight.', 'Reversed, confusion lifts, or a fear turns out smaller than it looked.', 'What am I afraid of that I have not checked in daylight?'),
 ('the-sun', 19, 'The Sun', 'joy, clarity, success', 'A child rides a white horse under a blazing sun. It is one of the warmest cards in the deck: things are clear, energy is high, and good news is easy to see.', 'Reversed, the joy is still there but dimmed, delayed or not fully let in.', 'What is going well that I have not let myself enjoy?'),
 ('judgement', 20, 'Judgement', 'awakening, reckoning, a call', 'An angel blows a trumpet and figures rise to answer. The card is about taking stock and answering a call you cannot ignore anymore.', 'Reversed, self-criticism gets in the way, or the call is heard and not answered.', 'What have I been called to do more than once?'),
 ('the-world', 21, 'The World', 'completion, wholeness, arrival', 'A dancer moves inside a wreath, with four figures in the corners. It marks the end of a full cycle: a project finished, a goal reached, a chapter complete.', 'Reversed, the last step is missing, or closure is being put off.', 'What would it take to call this chapter finished?'),
]
